Subject: [PATCH] docs(faq): explain how to avoid bumping a dependency with the
 same version

When a dependency is pinned to the same version as the project, a
version_files entry such as "pyproject.toml:version" also bumps the
dependency. Document the two ways to avoid it: use a version provider,
or anchor the pattern with "^". Add a test covering the anchored pattern.

Closes #819
---
 docs/faq.md                                | 43 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 tests/test_bump_update_version_in_files.py | 33 +++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 76 insertions(+)

diff --git a/docs/faq.md b/docs/faq.md
index 568ec2f..00a93e0 100644
--- a/docs/faq.md
+++ b/docs/faq.md
@@ -89,3 +89,46 @@ ignored_tag_formats = [
     "v${major}.${minor}",
 ]
 ```
+
+## How to avoid bumping a dependency that has the same version as my project?
+
+Each entry in [`version_files`](config/bump.md#version_files) is checked line by line:
+on every line that matches the entry's pattern, all occurrences of the current version are replaced.
+Without a pattern, every line containing the current version matches.
+
+So with `version_files = ["pyproject.toml:version"]`, a dependency pinned to the same version as your project
+is bumped as well, because its line also contains `version`:
+
+```toml
+[tool.poetry]
+version = "1.2.3"
+
+[tool.poetry.dependencies]
+foo = { version = "1.2.3", extras = ["bar"] }  # also changed to the new version
+```
+
+There are two ways to avoid this.
+
+**Use a [version provider](config/version_provider.md) (recommended).**
+If the version lives in a file that has a provider (e.g. `pep621`, `poetry`, `uv`, `cargo`, `npm` or `composer`),
+let the provider update it and remove that file from `version_files`.
+The provider only writes the project's own version field.
+
+```toml
+[tool.commitizen]
+version_provider = "poetry"
+```
+
+**Anchor the pattern.**
+The pattern is a regular expression matched against each line, so `^` anchors it to the start of the line:
+
+```toml
+[tool.commitizen]
+version_files = ["pyproject.toml:^version"]
+```
+
+Only lines starting with `version` are updated now.
+A line such as `version = "1.2.3"` inside a dependency table (e.g. `[tool.poetry.dependencies.foo]`) still matches,
+so make the pattern more specific if your file contains one.
+
+See [#496](https://github.com/commitizen-tools/commitizen/issues/496) for the original discussion.
diff --git a/tests/test_bump_update_version_in_files.py b/tests/test_bump_update_version_in_files.py
index d52725b..6e8ee0f 100644
--- a/tests/test_bump_update_version_in_files.py
+++ b/tests/test_bump_update_version_in_files.py
@@ -398,3 +398,36 @@ def test_update_version_in_files_return_value_partial_update(tmp_path):
     # Verify file2 was not changed
     content2 = file2.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
     assert content2 == "some other content\n"
+
+
+@pytest.mark.parametrize(
+    ("regex", "dependency_bumped"),
+    [
+        ("version", True),
+        ("^version", False),
+    ],
+)
+def test_anchored_regex_skips_dependency_with_same_version(
+    tmp_path, regex, dependency_bumped
+):
+    pyproject = tmp_path / "pyproject.toml"
+    pyproject.write_text(
+        '[tool.poetry]\nversion = "1.2.3"\n\n'
+        '[tool.poetry.dependencies]\nfoo = { version = "1.2.3", extras = ["bar"] }\n',
+        encoding="utf-8",
+    )
+
+    bump.update_version_in_files(
+        "1.2.3",
+        "2.0.0",
+        [f"{pyproject}:{regex}"],
+        check_consistency=False,
+        encoding="utf-8",
+    )
+
+    dependency_version = "2.0.0" if dependency_bumped else "1.2.3"
+    assert pyproject.read_text(encoding="utf-8") == (
+        '[tool.poetry]\nversion = "2.0.0"\n\n'
+        "[tool.poetry.dependencies]\n"
+        f'foo = {{ version = "{dependency_version}", extras = ["bar"] }}\n'
+    )
